1. The phrase ''Equality before law'' is taken from the British Constitution.
2. The phrase ''Equal protection of laws'' is taken from the American Constitution.
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?', '{"verdict": "**Option C is the correct answer**.", "core_concept": "Fundamental Rights: Right to Equality (Article 14).", "elimination_wisdom": "Article 14 embodies two concepts: ''Equality before law'' (negative concept), which is of British origin; and ''Equal protection of laws'' (positive concept), which is of American origin. Both statements are correct.", "trap_alerts": "Remember the sources of both concepts under Article 14 and their meanings (positive vs negative)."}', 60, 'standard', NULL, NULL, '{"layoutType":"statements","contextStr":"With reference to the Constitution of India, consider the following statements:","contextStrEn":"With reference to the Constitution of India, consider the following statements:","listItems":[{"id":"1","text":"The phrase ''Equality before law'' is taken from the British Constitution.","textEn":"The phrase ''Equality before law'' is taken from the British Constitution."},{"id":"2","text":"The phrase ''Equal protection of laws'' is taken from the American Constitution.","textEn":"The phrase ''Equal protection of laws'' is taken from the American Constitution."}],"questionPrompt":"Which of the statements given above is/are correct?","questionPromptEn":"Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"}', 'upsc');
